Let me analyze the Python code to translate to C++.

Key observations:

1. `BoyerMooreSearch` class with `text`, `pattern`, `textLen`, `patLen` members.
2. `match_in_pattern(char)`: scans pattern from right to left, returns index of char or -1.
3. `mismatch_in_text(currentPos)`: scans pattern from right to left, comparing `pattern[i] != text[currentPos + i]`, returns `currentPos + i` on mismatch or -1.
4. `bad_character_heuristic()`: iterates `i` from 0 to `textLen - patLen` inclusive. Note the Python quirk: reassigning `i` inside the loop doesn't affect the for-loop iteration (the loop variable is reassigned each iteration by `range`). So the `i = (mismatch_index - match_index)` assignment is dead code effectively — the loop continues with `i+1` from range regardless. So in C++, I must replicate that: a plain for loop where modifying `i` inside... wait, in C++ `for (int i = 0; i <= textLen - patLen; i++)` — if I assign `i = mismatch_index - match_index` inside the body, then `i++` happens, changing the loop behavior. That would NOT be identical to Python.

In Python, `for i in range(...)`: the assignment `i = ...` inside the loop body only affects the value during that iteration (visible after the body but before next iteration's reassignment); the next value comes from the range iterator, not from incrementing the assigned `i`. So the C++ equivalent must not change loop progression. So I should use a loop where I assign to a separate variable, or just not do the assignment at all since it's dead. To preserve behavior identically, the assignment is behaviorally dead (i is overwritten by next range value; also no side effects). So I can either keep a local variable assignment `int j = mismatch_index - match_index; (void)j;` or just omit. Cleanest: replicate with a comment or compute into a local. Since the instruction says keep behavior identical — the assignment has no observable effect, so omitting is fine. But to be safe/faithful, I could do:

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < textLen - patLen + 1; i++) {
    ...
    else {
        int match_index = match_in_pattern(text[mismatch_index]);
        (void)(mismatch_index - match_index); // Python rebinds loop var; no effect on range iteration
    }
}
```

Actually simpler: just call match_in_pattern and discard the result assignment. But calling match_in_pattern could matter? It has no side effects other than computation. But the mismatch_index - match_index computation — no side effects. So we can even skip the call. But to be faithful to behavior including "side effects" — there are none. However, edge cases: `self.text[mismatch_index]` — mismatch_index is guaranteed valid (within text). match_in_pattern could receive out-of-range char? char is a char; in C++ we pass char. Fine.

Edge case: `range(self.textLen - self.patLen + 1)` — if patLen > textLen, range is empty or negative range → empty. In C++, `i < textLen - patLen + 1` with ints: if textLen - patLen + 1 is negative, loop doesn't execute since i=0 not < negative. Good.

Edge case: `mismatch_in_text` reads `text[currentPos + i]` — with i up to patLen-1, currentPos up to textLen-patLen, so index up to textLen-1. Safe.

`match_in_pattern` returns int.

Constructor: stores strings by value.

C++ code:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <vector>

class BoyerMooreSearch {
public:
    BoyerMooreSearch(std::string text, std::string pattern)
        : text(std::move(text)), pattern(std::move(pattern)),
          textLen(this->text.size()), patLen(this->pattern.size()) {}

    int match_in_pattern(char char_) const {
        for (int i = patLen - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
            if (char_ == pattern[i]) {
                return i;
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }

    int mismatch_in_text(int currentPos) const {
        for (int i = patLen - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
            if (pattern[i] != text[currentPos + i]) {
                return currentPos + i;
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }

    std::vector<int> bad_character_heuristic() const {
        std::vector<int> positions;
        for (int i = 0; i < textLen - patLen + 1; ++i) {
            int mismatch_index = mismatch_in_text(i);
            if (mismatch_index == -1) {
                positions.push_back(i);
            } else {
                int match_index = match_in_pattern(text[mismatch_index]);
                // In Python, rebinding the loop variable `i` here does not
                // affect the range iteration, so this value is unused.
                (void)(mismatch_index - match_index);
            }
        }
        return positions;
    }

private:
    std::string text;
    std::string pattern;
    int textLen;
    int patLen;
};
```

